{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 4,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.138,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "206"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.104,
            0.9,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.139,
            0.901,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": "Are the events E and F in the example above elementary events? No, they are **not** because the event E has 2 outcomes and the event F has 4 outcomes."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.185,
            0.51,
            0.204
          ],
          "content": "**Remarks :** From Example 1, we note that"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.249,
            0.213,
            0.897,
            0.254
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm { P ( E )   +   P ( F )   =   \\frac { 1 } { 2 }   +   \\frac { 1 } { 2 }   =   1 }\\tag{1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_4_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.259,
            0.772,
            0.279
          ],
          "content": "where E is the event ‘getting a head’ and F is the event ‘getting a tail’."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.285,
            0.562,
            0.305
          ],
          "content": "From (i) and (ii) of Example 3, we also get"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.317,
            0.896,
            0.358
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm { P ( E ) + P ( F ) = } { \\frac { 1 } { 3 } } + { \\frac { 2 } { 3 } } = 1\\tag{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_4_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.369,
            0.896,
            0.389
          ],
          "content": "where E is the event ‘getting a number ${ > } 4 ^ { \\circ }$ and F is the event ‘getting a number $\\leq 4 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.396,
            0.901,
            0.434
          ],
          "content": "Note that getting a number not greater than 4 is same as getting a number less than or equal to 4, and vice versa."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.441,
            0.902,
            0.485
          ],
          "content": "In (1) and (2) above, is F not the same as ‘not $\\mathrm { E } ^ { \\prime } ?$ Yes, it is. We denote the event ‘not $\\mathrm { E ^ { \\prime } }$ by $\\overline { { \\overline { { \\mathrm { E } } } } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.488,
            0.413,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": "So, P(E) + P(not E) = 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.516,
            0.772,
            0.538
          ],
          "content": "i.e., $\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{E}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\overline{\\mathrm{E}}) \\equiv 1;$ which gives us P( E ) = 1 – P(E)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.544,
            0.58,
            0.564
          ],
          "content": "In general, it is true that for an event $\\mathbf { E } _ { z }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.301,
            0.571,
            0.487,
            0.592
          ],
          "content": "\\mathbf{P}(\\mathbf{E}) = \\mathbf{1} - \\mathbf{P}(\\mathbf{E})",
          "image_source": "images/page_4_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.6,
            0.899,
            0.647
          ],
          "content": "The event $\\overline { \\mathrm { E } }$ , representing ‘not E’, is called the **complement** of the event E. We also say that E and $\\overline { { \\overline { { \\mathrm { E } } } } }$ are **complementary** events."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.653,
            0.901,
            0.674
          ],
          "content": "Before proceeding further, let us try to find the answers to the following questions:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.679,
            0.831,
            0.699
          ],
          "content": "(i) What is the probability of getting a number 8 in a single throw of a die?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.706,
            0.901,
            0.753
          ],
          "content": "(ii) What is the probability of getting a number less than 7 in a single throw of a die? **Let us answer (i) :**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.762,
            0.902,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": "We know that there are only six possible outcomes in a single throw of a die. These outcomes are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6. Since no face of the die is marked 8, so there is no outcome favourable to 8, i.e., the number of such outcomes is zero. In other words, getting 8 in a single throw of a die, is impossible."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.868,
            0.135,
            0.887
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.249,
            0.855,
            0.464,
            0.896
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{getting}\\ 8)=\\frac{0}{6}=0",
          "image_source": "images/page_4_equation_21.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.563,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
